Dictionary:
A dictionary is a mutable data structure that stores data as key-value pairs. 
Keys are unique and immutable, values can be any data type, and most operations like search, insert, and delete take O(1) time on average.

Definition: Stores data in Key : Value pairs.
Mutable:Can add, update, and delete items.
Ordered:Maintains insertion order (Python 3.7+).
Keys: Must be unique and immutable (int, str, tuple).
Values: Can be of any data type and can repeat.
Access: dict[key] or dict.get(key)
Add/Update: dict[key] = value, update()
Delete: del, pop(), clear()
Traversal: keys(), values(), items()
Search/Insert/Delete: O(1) average (uses a hash table).

Dictionary
│
├── Key : Value
├── Mutable 
├── Ordered (3.7+)
├── Keys → Unique + Immutable
├── Values → Any Type + Duplicates Allowed
├── Access → [] / get()
├── Methods → keys(), values(), items(), update(), pop()
└── Time → O(1) Search, Insert, Delete

| Method      | Purpose                    |
| ----------- | -------------------------- |
| `keys()`    | Returns all keys           |
| `values()`  | Returns all values         |
| `items()`   | Returns key-value pairs    |
| `get()`     | Safe access                |
| `update()`  | Merge/update dictionary    |
| `pop()`     | Remove a key               |
| `popitem()` | Removes last inserted item |
| `clear()`   | Removes everything         |
| `copy()`    | Creates shallow copy       |
